======>write down step to create S3 bucket in cloud computing 2 TIMES 5MARK
1. Sign in to AWS Console
  • Go to: https://console.aws.amazon.com
  • Log in using your IAM user or root account credentials.
2. Go to the S3 Service
  • In the AWS Management Console, search for “S3” in the search bar.
  • Click on S3 under Storage services.
3. Click on “Create bucket”
  • This button is at the top-right of the S3 dashboard.
4. Configure Bucket Settings
 General Settings:
  • Bucket name: Enter a unique name globally (e.g., my-data-bucket-2025)
  • Region: Select the AWS Region where you want to create the bucket (e.g., US-East-1)
5. Configure Options (Optional)
You can leave defaults or configure:
  • Versioning: Enable if you want to keep multiple versions of objects.
  • Tags: Add key-value pairs to organize your bucket.
6. Set Permissions
  • By default, S3 blocks public access. You can:
  o Keep it blocked for private data (recommended)
  o Modify for public access (e.g., static website hosting)
7. Review and Create
  • Review all your settings.
  • Click on “Create bucket” at the bottom.


======>write down step to create amazon VPC in cloud computing 2 TIMES 5 MARK
1. Sign in to AWS Console
  • Go to https://console.aws.amazon.com
  • Log in using your IAM user or root account credentials.
2. Open the VPC Dashboard
  • In the AWS Management Console, search for “VPC” in the search bar.
  • Click on VPC under Networking & Content Delivery to open the VPC dashboard.
3. Click on “Create VPC”
  • In the VPC Dashboard, click the “Create VPC” button at the top-right.
4. Configure the VPC Settings
 VPC Configuration:
  • Name tag: Provide a name for your VPC (e.g., MyVPC).
  • IPv4 CIDR block: Define the range of IP addresses for the VPC in CIDR notation (e.g.,
    10.0.0.0/16). This defines the size of your network.
5. Configure Additional Settings
  • DNS resolution: Keep the default setting enabled to allow instances in the VPC to resolve
    domain names.
  • DNS hostnames: Enable this if you want instances in the VPC to have public DNS names.
6. Review and Create
  • After configuring, click on the “Create VPC” button to create the VPC.
  • AWS will then provision your VPC and you’ll see a confirmation screen with the VPC details.
7. Set Up Networking Components (Optional)
Now that your VPC is created, you might want to set up essential networking components like:
  • Subnets: Divide your VPC’s IP range into smaller blocks and assign them to specific
    availability zones (AZs).
  • Internet Gateway (IGW): If you want your VPC to communicate with the internet, create and
    attach an Internet Gateway to your VPC.
8. Optional: Create a VPC Peering Connection or VPN
  • You can create a VPC Peering Connection to allow communication between different VPCs.
  • You can also set up a VPN Gateway to connect your VPC to an on-premises data center.



========>write step for website hosting in cloud computing
1. Sign in to AWS Management Console
  • Go to the AWS Console at https://aws.amazon.com.
  • Log in with your AWS credentials (IAM user or root account).
2. Create an S3 Bucket
  • Go to S3: In the AWS Management Console, search for S3 and select it from the services list.
  • Create Bucket: Click on Create Bucket.
3. Enable Static Website Hosting
  • After the bucket is created, select the bucket name from the S3 dashboard.
  • Go to the "Properties" tab and scroll down to Static website hosting.
4. Upload Website Files
  • Go to the "Objects" tab in your bucket.
  • Click "Upload" and choose your website files (e.g., index.html, style.css, script.js, etc.).
5. Set Bucket Permissions for Public Access
  • Go to the "Permissions" tab in the bucket.
6. Test the Website URL
  • After enabling static website hosting and uploading files, AWS will provide you with a
    website endpoint URL.
7. Optional: Use a Custom Domain (Route 53)
  • If you want to use a custom domain (like www.example.com), you'll need to configure
    Amazon Route 53 or another DNS service to point your domain to your S3 bucket.
8. Enable HTTPS with Amazon CloudFront (Optional)
  • For better security, you can enable SSL/TLS encryption using Amazon CloudFront.
  o Create a CloudFront distribution with your S3 bucket as the origin.
  o Set up SSL certificates via AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) to serve the website over
HTTPS


=======>write down to step to create Alarm in cloud computing 2 TIMES 5 MARKS
Step 1: Sign in to the AWS Management Console
  • Open your web browser and go to the AWS Management Console.
  • Sign in using your AWS account credentials.
Step 2: Access Amazon CloudWatch
  • In the AWS Management Console, type "CloudWatch" into the search bar at the top and
    select CloudWatch from the search results.
  • This will take you to the CloudWatch Dashboard.
Step 3: Navigate to Alarms
  • In the left-hand menu under the CloudWatch section, select "Alarms".
  • Click on the "Create Alarm" button located at the top of the page.
Step 4: Select a Metric to Monitor
  • You’ll be prompted to choose a metric that you want to monitor for the alarm.
  • Choose a metric by selecting the appropriate service, such as EC2, EBS, Lambda, RDS, or any
    custom metric.
Step 5: Define the Alarm Condition
  • Set Threshold: Now you’ll define the threshold that triggers the alarm.
Step 6: Configure Actions
  • Select Actions that will occur when the alarm state changes. You can choose to:
  o Send Notification: Choose an existing SNS (Simple Notification Service) topic or
    create a new one to receive email alerts when the alarm state is triggered. You can
    add multiple email addresses or other communication methods.
Step 7: Add a Name and Description
  • Name the Alarm: Provide a unique name for the alarm (e.g., “High CPU Utilization Alarm”).
Step 8: Review and Create the Alarm
  • Review all the settings for your alarm:
  o Ensure the metric and threshold are correctly set.
Step 9: Monitor the Alarm
  • Once the alarm is created, it will be visible in the Alarms section of the CloudWatch
    dashboard.
Step 10: Modify or Delete the Alarm (Optional)
  • If you need to modify or delete the alarm, go back to the Alarms section in CloudWatch.
